WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:06.130
Hey David, seriously, what is wrong with compulsive
liars like Trump and real life and other people

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:07.730
that I know in real life?

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.400
Is it a medical condition?

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.269
So there's actually quite a bit of litter
literature about this.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:18.930
Um, pathological or compulsive lying is a
condition that is named and you know, that

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:21.500
doesn't tell us anything about it one way
or the other.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.660
You can argue that we have overmedicalization
in our society where things that should not

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.370
really be diagnosable conditions are diagnosable
conditions, but there is a condition called

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:32.619
pathological lying.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:36.520
It's the behavior of habitual or, or compulsive
lying.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.940
Uh, some psychiatrists distinguish pathological
lying and compulsive lying.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.900
Others say that it's the same thing.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:50.970
Um, but the idea is, uh, that um, there is
a medicalization of this phenomena which not

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.430
everybody necessarily agrees with.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.700
It's a standalone disorder.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:02.810
It is often also a symptom of other conditions
like psychopathy and antisocial personality

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.600
disorder often will, uh, have compulsive or
pathological lying.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.659
Narcissists often lie compulsively.

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:14.990
People with histrionic personality disorder
often will have pathological or compulsive

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:15.990
lying.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:20.010
Um, the average onset is 16 years.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:26.220
And for people who have above average intelligence,
uh, at least average or above average, they

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:32.360
tend to be more likely to develop this condition
of pathological or compulsive lying.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.479
Now, if we want to reject for a second that
this is merely a, uh, something to medicalize

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:41.979
name and then look at which pill can deal
with it.

00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:45.810
I've dealt with a lot of, uh, compulsive liars
in my life.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.010
I've told some of these stories before.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:52.850
In a high school, I worked with people who
were compulsive liars and they would lie about

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.939
things much like Trump actually, where you
wonder, why would you lie about something,

00:01:56.939 --> 00:01:58.530
number one, so insignificant.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.159
And number two, so easily falsifiable.

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:07.079
I vividly remember a girl that I knew who
said that she had, you know, $600 glasses

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:08.079
or something like that.

00:02:08.079 --> 00:02:11.480
And at the time I remember thinking, I know
that you don't, I know that, you know, at

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:16.319
the time, based on what things cost at the
time, nobody our age has $600 glasses.

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:17.860
Your glasses clearly aren't special.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.830
They're normal glasses from the place down
the street.

00:02:20.830 --> 00:02:25.459
But I didn't really understand what it was
about that that was appealing to her.

00:02:25.459 --> 00:02:31.470
Why was it appealing to lie and claim that
she had $600 glasses and it probably connected

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:38.370
to some, uh, insufficiency that she felt elsewhere,
which she was trying to make up for with the

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:43.810
totally false notion that she owned this really
expensive thing that maybe people would find

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:44.810
interesting.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.020
I, my reaction when I meet compulsive liars
and I've met a lot of them, is I don't even

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.930
react with any kind of surprise to the lies
they tell.

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:58.209
Because what I've sort of learned from experience
is that oftentimes the compulsive liar is

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:02.209
looking to generate a reaction and they feed
off of that reaction.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:07.630
And they notice that when they tell these
lies, a lot of other people will react and

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:11.640
they will become more interested in the person
or they will react visibly in a way that sort

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:14.629
of fuels the person's desire to tell more
of these stories.

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:19.210
So I've dealt with people who say, yeah, it's
a, I actually, yeah, I mean I just can't,

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.540
I can't even think now, but there's so many
examples.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.830
I just got back from a trip.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.700
They actually got brought over to Japan.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:28.570
I just spent two weeks there because I go
over there and do speeches and they, they

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:33.280
pay me 50 grand to speech and it's really
great and I know that the person has no money

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.209
and I know that they can barely afford rent.

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:39.159
And I know that they were asking another friend
of mine whether they could maybe move in together.

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.569
I don't even react.

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:41.569
Like I'm impressed.

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:42.739
I say, wow, that's really great.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:44.710
Hmm, that's interesting.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.550
And then I just move on to something else
that actually interests me.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:51.940
And this will often, what really happens is
the person just stops being interested in

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:56.920
talking to me because I'm not giving them
the feedback that they want.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.330
So that's my experience.

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:03.879
I find the compulsive lying very toxic, although
I am completely open to the idea that it connects

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:07.410
to childhood trauma or other conditions or
whatever.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.209
I'm not saying compulsive liars or bad people
or anything like that, but for me, for the

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:14.890
way I like to live my life and the people
I like to be around, I just ignore the line

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.010
completely.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:20.340
Pretend like everything is not only true that
they're saying, but so unremarkable that it's

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.020
like if they said, I bought, I bought a bananas
this morning.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.020
Oh wow.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:25.020
That's interesting.

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.020
Yeah, that's it.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:29.430
I'm going to move on to something that's interesting
to me and typically they will distance themselves

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:34.229
because they want to be around people who
will react in the way that they like that

00:04:34.229 --> 00:04:37.400
feeds the lying and often gets them to tell
more lies.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.729
Let me know your experiences with compulsive
or pathological liars.

00:04:41.729 --> 00:04:43.000
Interested to hear your take.

